#: ../../docs/tutorials/ui/creating_applications.rst:4
msgid "Creating applications"
msgstr ""

#: ../../docs/tutorials/ui/creating_applications.rst:6
msgid "Godot features an extensive built-in UI system, and its small distribution size can make it a suitable alternative to frameworks like Electron or Qt."
msgstr ""

#: ../../docs/tutorials/ui/creating_applications.rst:9
msgid "This page provides guidelines for creating non-game applications with Godot, as well as instructions on performing common tasks to improve desktop integration."
msgstr ""

#: ../../docs/tutorials/ui/creating_applications.rst:14
msgid "Godot is a game engine first and foremost. This means that creating applications with Godot is a byproduct of its feature set, and not its primary focus of development."
msgstr ""

#: ../../docs/tutorials/ui/creating_applications.rst:20
#: ../../docs/tutorials/ui/creating_applications.rst:724
msgid "Check out `Material Maker <https://github.com/RodZill4/material-maker>`__ and `Pixelorama <https://github.com/Orama-Interactive/Pixelorama>`__ for examples of open source applications made with Godot."
msgstr ""

#: ../../docs/tutorials/ui/creating_applications.rst:25
msgid "Performing common tasks"
msgstr ""

#: ../../docs/tutorials/ui/creating_applications.rst:28
msgid "Spawning multiple windows"
msgstr ""

#: ../../docs/tutorials/ui/creating_applications.rst:30
msgid "*This is only supported on Windows, macOS, and Linux (X11/XWayland only, not in native Wayland mode).*"
msgstr ""

#: ../../docs/tutorials/ui/creating_applications.rst:33
msgid "Additional windows can be created using the :ref:`class_Window` node. Windows can be moved, resized, minimized, and closed independently from the main application window."
msgstr ""

#: ../../docs/tutorials/ui/creating_applications.rst:37
msgid "However, if you close the main window, all other windows will also be closed since closing the main window ends the process. You can avoid this by minimizing the main window, setting its :ref:`unfocusable <class_Window_property_unfocusable>` property to ``true`` (to hide it from the taskbar and task switcher), then creating additional Window nodes immediately on startup. Remember to provide an alternative means of exiting the application in this case, such as a :ref:`tray icon <doc_creating_applications_tray_icon>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../docs/tutorials/ui/creating_applications.rst:46
msgid "Constraining the window size"
msgstr ""

#: ../../docs/tutorials/ui/creating_applications.rst:48
msgid "Most applications can only render correctly starting from a certain minimum window size. For more specific use cases, it may also be desired to force a maximum window size."
msgstr ""

#: ../../docs/tutorials/ui/creating_applications.rst:52
msgid "Size limits can be enforced using the :ref:`min_size <class_Window_property_min_size>` and :ref:`max_size <class_Window_property_max_size>` properties on a Window node. Remember to multiply these size limits according to the application's scale factor (see :ref:`doc_creating_applications_scaling_to_hidpi_displays` for details)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../docs/tutorials/ui/creating_applications.rst:59
msgid "As a reminder, you can retrieve the root Window node to set properties on it using :ref:`get_window() <class_Node_method_get_window>` on any Node."
msgstr ""

#: ../../docs/tutorials/ui/creating_applications.rst:63
msgid "Using native file dialogs"
msgstr ""

#: ../../docs/tutorials/ui/creating_applications.rst:65
msgid "*This is only supported on Windows, macOS, Linux, and Android.*"
msgstr ""

#: ../../docs/tutorials/ui/creating_applications.rst:67
msgid "By default, Godot uses its own :ref:`class_FileDialog` implementation for file dialogs. However, you can use the operating system's native file dialogs instead. This is generally preferred by users, as native file dialogs better integrate with the desktop environment and provide a more familiar experience."
msgstr ""

#: ../../docs/tutorials/ui/creating_applications.rst:72
msgid "You can opt into native file dialogs by enabling the :ref:`use_native_dialog <class_FileDialog_property_use_native_dialog>` property on the FileDialog node. This must be done on each FileDialog node used in the project, as there is no project setting to control this behavior globally."
msgstr ""

#: ../../docs/tutorials/ui/creating_applications.rst:78
#: ../../docs/tutorials/ui/creating_applications.rst:82
msgid "Comparison between standard FileDialog (left) and native file dialog (right) on macOS"
msgstr ""

#: ../../docs/tutorials/ui/creating_applications.rst:86
msgid "See the :ref:`property description <class_FileDialog_property_use_native_dialog>` for details on platform support."
msgstr ""

#: ../../docs/tutorials/ui/creating_applications.rst:89
msgid "Additionally, on macOS, native file dialogs are not supported when game embedding is enabled in the editor. To test this functionality when running the project, make sure you disable game embedding by switching to the :menu:`Game` screen, clicking the rightmost icon in the bar at the top and unchecking :menu:`Embed Game on Next Play`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../docs/tutorials/ui/creating_applications.rst:98
msgid "Creating an icon in the system tray"
msgstr ""

#: ../../docs/tutorials/ui/creating_applications.rst:100
#: ../../docs/tutorials/ui/creating_applications.rst:302
msgid "*This is only supported on Windows and macOS.*"
msgstr ""

#: ../../docs/tutorials/ui/creating_applications.rst:102
msgid "You can create one or more icons in the system tray (also known as the notification area) using a :ref:`class_StatusIndicator` node. In addition to a tooltip, this node can have a :ref:`class_PopupMenu` node assigned, so that a dropdown can be shown when clicking the icon."
msgstr ""

#: ../../docs/tutorials/ui/creating_applications.rst:107
msgid "StatusIndicator also has a :ref:`pressed <class_StatusIndicator_signal_pressed>` signal that is emitted when the icon is clicked. Use this to perform an action without making a dropdown show up, or perform different actions depending on which mouse button was pressed."
msgstr ""

#: ../../docs/tutorials/ui/creating_applications.rst:112
msgid "After creating a tray icon, you may also want to implement \"minimize on close\" behavior. This means that when the user attempts to close the application using the window manager X button, it will minimize to the tray instead. To do so, attach this script to an :ref:`Autoload <doc_singletons_autoload>` *scene* with a StatusIndicator as the root node:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../docs/tutorials/ui/creating_applications.rst:159
msgid "See :ref:`doc_handling_quit_requests` for details on overriding the behavior when the user tries to close the application. This is important to handle when the user has unsaved changes to avoid data loss."
msgstr ""

#: ../../docs/tutorials/ui/creating_applications.rst:165
msgid "When multiple StatusIndicator nodes are present, their order in the system tray is determined by the order in which they are added to the scene tree."
msgstr ""

#: ../../docs/tutorials/ui/creating_applications.rst:170
msgid "Using the global menu"
msgstr ""

#: ../../docs/tutorials/ui/creating_applications.rst:172
#: ../../docs/tutorials/ui/creating_applications.rst:234
msgid "*This is only supported on macOS.*"
msgstr ""

#: ../../docs/tutorials/ui/creating_applications.rst:174
msgid "On macOS, applications can use the system's global menu bar instead of displaying a menu bar inside the application window. This is also referred to as a *native menu* in Godot."
msgstr ""

#: ../../docs/tutorials/ui/creating_applications.rst:178
#: ../../docs/tutorials/ui/creating_applications.rst:182
msgid "Comparison between standard MenuBar (left), MenuBar with native popups (middle), and native menu (right) on macOS"
msgstr ""

#: ../../docs/tutorials/ui/creating_applications.rst:184
msgid "Godot supports creating menus through the :ref:`class_MenuBar` node, which displays its :ref:`class_PopupMenu` children as menus. You can enable the global menu support on a given MenuBar node by enabling its :ref:`prefer_global_menu <class_MenuBar_property_prefer_global_menu>` property in the inspector. On macOS, this will cause the MenuBar node to disappear and take no space, with its menus being displayed in the system's global menu bar instead. If this property is disabled, the MenuBar node will display its menus inside the application window as usual, but native popups are still used when supported by the operating system."
msgstr ""

#: ../../docs/tutorials/ui/creating_applications.rst:196
msgid "The app menu (with the project name in bold), as well as the :menu:`Window` and :menu:`Help` menus are always present on macOS. You should not add these to the global menu manually."
msgstr ""

#: ../../docs/tutorials/ui/creating_applications.rst:200
msgid "In Godot 4.6 and later, you can add new items within these menus by changing the :ref:`system_menu_id <class_PopupMenu_property_system_menu_id>` property on the PopupMenu node. You can choose between **Application Menu** (the first menu with the application name in bold), **Window Menu**, **Help Menu** and **Dock** (shown when right-clicking the icon in the Dock). Standard menu items that are already present in those menus will be preserved:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../docs/tutorials/ui/creating_applications.rst:207
#: ../../docs/tutorials/ui/creating_applications.rst:211
msgid "Custom options added to the system Window menu on macOS"
msgstr ""

#: ../../docs/tutorials/ui/creating_applications.rst:213
msgid "A project can have multiple MenuBar nodes. If multiple MenuBar nodes have the **Prefer Global Menu** property enabled, the menu options will be added at the index defined by the **Start Index** property when the MenuBar node is added to the scene tree. This allows putting context-specific menus at the end of the menu bar, so that the first menu options stay in place when the additional menu bar is added or removed."
msgstr ""

#: ../../docs/tutorials/ui/creating_applications.rst:220
msgid "For more advanced use cases, you can also use the :ref:`class_NativeMenu` singleton directly without using the MenuBar node."
msgstr ""

#: ../../docs/tutorials/ui/creating_applications.rst:225
msgid "Global menu integration is not supported when game embedding is enabled in the editor. To test this functionality when running the project, make sure you disable game embedding by switching to the :menu:`Game` screen, clicking the rightmost icon in the bar at the top and unchecking :menu:`Embed Game on Next Play`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../docs/tutorials/ui/creating_applications.rst:232
msgid "Using client-side decorations"
msgstr ""

#: ../../docs/tutorials/ui/creating_applications.rst:236
msgid "Many modern applications use *client-side decorations* (CSD) instead of relying on the operating system's window manager to draw the title bar and window borders (server-side decorations). This allows for a more customizable appearance and better integration with the application's UI."
msgstr ""

#: ../../docs/tutorials/ui/creating_applications.rst:241
msgid "Godot currently only supports client-side decorations on macOS. This can be used by enabling the :ref:`display/window/size/extend_to_title <class_ProjectSettings_property_display/window/size/extend_to_title>` project setting."
msgstr ""

#: ../../docs/tutorials/ui/creating_applications.rst:246
#: ../../docs/tutorials/ui/creating_applications.rst:250
msgid "Comparison between standard window decorations (top) and client-side decorations (bottom) on macOS"
msgstr ""

#: ../../docs/tutorials/ui/creating_applications.rst:252
msgid "After enabling client-side decorations, the window border will no longer display, and minimize/maximize/close buttons will show as an overlay to the application. You need to make sure the application provides enough of a margin at the top for the buttons to display comfortably, while also displaying the window title using a Label node or similar."
msgstr ""

#: ../../docs/tutorials/ui/creating_applications.rst:258
msgid "To conditionally adapt your UI according to whether client-side decorations are enabled, use :ref:`DisplayServer.has_feature <class_DisplayServer_method_has_feature>` and also check the current value of :ref:`Window.extend_to_title <class_Window_property_extend_to_title>` (which is what the project setting changes):"
msgstr ""

#: ../../docs/tutorials/ui/creating_applications.rst:276
msgid "To correctly position the window title, consider using :ref:`DisplayServer.window_get_safe_title_margins() <class_DisplayServer_method_window_get_safe_title_margins>` which returns a Vector3 where ``x`` is the left margin, ``y`` is the right margin (will increase when the system uses right-to-left typesetting), and ``z`` is the height. Additionally, you can call :ref:`DisplayServer.window_set_window_buttons_offset() <class_DisplayServer_method_window_set_window_buttons_offset>` to adjust the position of the close/minimize/maximize buttons (usually to vertically center them)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../docs/tutorials/ui/creating_applications.rst:285
#: ../../docs/tutorials/ui/creating_applications.rst:289
msgid "Safe title margins when using client-side decorations on macOS"
msgstr ""

#: ../../docs/tutorials/ui/creating_applications.rst:293
msgid "On macOS, client-side decorations are not supported when game embedding is enabled in the editor. To test this functionality when running the project, make sure you disable game embedding by switching to the :menu:`Game` screen, clicking the rightmost icon in the bar at the top and unchecking :menu:`Embed Game on Next Play`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../docs/tutorials/ui/creating_applications.rst:300
msgid "Displaying progress status on the taskbar/Dock"
msgstr ""

#: ../../docs/tutorials/ui/creating_applications.rst:304
msgid "It is possible for applications to report a progress status to the operating system, which can be displayed on the taskbar or Dock icon. This status is composed of a state (active, paused, error) and a completion percentage. This can be used to show progress while the user is not focused on the application."
msgstr ""

#: ../../docs/tutorials/ui/creating_applications.rst:310
#: ../../docs/tutorials/ui/creating_applications.rst:314
msgid "Progress reporting in the Dock on macOS"
msgstr ""

#: ../../docs/tutorials/ui/creating_applications.rst:316
msgid "This is often accomplished by synchronizing a :ref:`class_ProgressBar` node's progress with the progress reported to the operating system:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../docs/tutorials/ui/creating_applications.rst:336
msgid "Several progress states are available: no progress (hides the progress bar), indeterminate, normal, paused, error. Check the class reference for details."
msgstr ""

#: ../../docs/tutorials/ui/creating_applications.rst:339
msgid "You can also use :ref:`Window.request_attention() <class_Window_method_request_attention>` to make the window flash in the taskbar (or bounce in the Dock on macOS). For example, this can be used to attract the user's attention after a long operation is completed."
msgstr ""

#: ../../docs/tutorials/ui/creating_applications.rst:347
msgid "Reporting progress is not supported when game embedding is enabled in the editor. To test this functionality when running the project, make sure you disable game embedding by switching to the :menu:`Game` screen, clicking the rightmost icon in the bar at the top and unchecking :menu:`Embed Game on Next Play`."
msgstr ""

